{
    "background_story": "Winter was a skilled and fearless medicine woman who lived in a small village on the outskirts of the Inca Empire. She was known for her expertise in the ancient practice of trephination, a delicate surgical procedure that involved removing a portion of the skull to relieve pressure on the brain. Winter's knowledge of trephination was passed down through generations of her family, and she had honed her skills through years of apprenticeship and practice. When a young warrior named Kanaq was brought to her with a severe head wound, Winter performed the intricate procedure with precision and care. Kanaq made a full recovery, and Winter's reputation as a master healer spread throughout the land. Her bravery and skill in the face of uncertainty earned her a revered place in the village, and her name became synonymous with trephination and the art of healing.",
